<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#gods-wife-of-amun">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">God's Wife of Amun</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Divine Adoratrice of Amun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">God's Wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> was the title of the highest-ranking priestess of the god Amun at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. From the Third Intermediate Period it was held by unmarried royal daughters, each adopting her successor, and the office controlled great temple wealth and political influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>. Kings secured Thebes by having their daughters adopted, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-i">Psamtik I</a> did with Nitocris in 656 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gods-wife-of-amun.jpg" alt="God's Wife of Amun" width="320" height="800" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: tutincommon (John Campana) · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Ankhenesneferibre_by_John_Campana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
